                                                "text": "a flying rumour"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "60222875-a3b8-4890-bf73-6eb4d356a3a3",
                                                "sense_id": "4cfe2c10-2fd9-32d2-9e16-1ad22d982371",
                                                "text": "in a sudden hail of exploding flying glass"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "38ba46ff-d0bb-45c0-b846-e459283192ed",
                                                "sense_id": "4cfe2c10-2fd9-32d2-9e16-1ad22d982371",
                                                "text": "Matthew (26—6 to 13), Mark (14—3 to 9), and Luke (7—37 and 38) also heard of, and related, the circumstance of Mary, whom John says (11 — 2) was the sister of Lazarus, anointing the head of Jesus with ointment, yet they neither of them utter a syllable about his raising her brother from the dead. It is difficult to account for this fact, unless we suppose that John was actually dishonest, or that he took up, believed and recorded a flying story, which an occurrence of some kind had given rise to, but which was without any foundation in truth."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
